## Error Summary

The application failed in `UserService.updateProfile()` because it could not obtain a JDBC connection from the pool before the timeout expired. The stack trace shows the failure originates in `ConnectionPool.getConnection(ConnectionPool.java:45)`, then bubbles up through `UserService.updateProfile(UserService.java:112)` and `UserController.handleUpdate(UserController.java:23)`. This is a classic connection-acquisition timeout, not a profile-update logic error. ([docs.oracle.com](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/javax/sql/CommonDataSource.html))

## Root Cause Analysis

The immediate root cause is that the app exhausted or could not refresh database connections fast enough, so `getConnection()` waited until the configured timeout and then failed. In JDBC terms, `setLoginTimeout()`/`DriverManager.getLoginTimeout()` control how long the driver or `DataSource` waits while attempting to connect to a database. HikariCP similarly exposes `connectionTimeout` as the maximum time a caller waits for a connection from the pool, and it also provides `leakDetectionThreshold` for spotting connections that are borrowed too long. ([docs.oracle.com](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/javax/sql/CommonDataSource.html))

The most likely underlying causes are:
1. **Pool exhaustion**: too many concurrent requests for the configured `maximumPoolSize`. HikariCP’s sizing guidance explicitly warns that pool sizing is often misconfigured and that bigger is not always better. ([github.com](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/wiki/About-Pool-Sizing))
2. **Connection leaks**: code paths that fail to close `Connection`, `Statement`, or `ResultSet`, which keeps connections checked out until the pool runs dry. Microsoft’s SQL Server guidance calls out failures to close pooled connections as a common reason for “timeout expired” / “obtaining a connection from the pool” errors. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/connect/timeout-expired-error))
3. **Slow or blocked database/network**: long-running queries, database overload, or network/TLS/handshake latency can prevent connections from being returned or established in time. Microsoft’s connectivity guidance notes that timeout errors occur when the server is not responding fast enough, and Oracle documents using login/connect timeouts when the database connection itself is slow. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/connect/timeout-expired-error))

## Step-by-Step Resolution

1. **Check whether connections are being closed correctly.**  
   Audit `UserService.updateProfile()` and every DAO/repository method it calls to ensure all JDBC resources are wrapped in `try-with-resources` so `Connection`, `PreparedStatement`, and `ResultSet` are always closed. This is the first thing to verify when the pool is timing out. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/connect/timeout-expired-error))

2. **Add leak detection temporarily.**  
   Enable HikariCP `leakDetectionThreshold` long enough to flag connections that are held too long, then inspect the logs for the stack trace of the borrower. HikariCP documents this as the mechanism for logging possible connection leaks. ([github.com](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/dev/README.md))

3. **Inspect pool saturation metrics.**  
   Look at active/idle/waiting connection counts. If `active == maximumPoolSize` and threads are waiting, you have pool exhaustion; if the pool is small for the request volume, tune `maximumPoolSize` carefully instead of increasing it blindly. HikariCP’s pool-sizing guidance emphasizes that connection pools are frequently misconfigured and that smaller, well-sized pools often perform better. ([github.com](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/wiki/About-Pool-Sizing))

4. **Verify database responsiveness.**  
   Check DB CPU, lock waits, slow queries, and network latency around `2026-07-11 11:15:30.452`. If the database is slow or unreachable, connection acquisition will time out even if the app code is correct. Oracle and Microsoft both document timeout-expired scenarios caused by slow or unresponsive server-side connectivity. ([docs.oracle.com](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/javax/sql/CommonDataSource.html))

5. **Tune timeout settings explicitly.**  
   Set:
   - `connectionTimeout` in HikariCP to a value aligned with your SLO,
   - JDBC `setLoginTimeout()` for connection establishment,
   - and, if relevant, driver socket/read timeouts so stalled network calls fail fast. HikariCP exposes `connectionTimeout`, and the JDBC API exposes `setLoginTimeout()` for connection attempts. ([github.com](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/dev/README.md))

6. **Validate max lifetime / keepalive settings.**  
   Ensure pool connection lifetime and keepalive settings are compatible with your database and network infrastructure so the pool does not accumulate dead connections. HikariCP explicitly recommends TCP keepalive to avoid rare “pool goes to zero and does not recover” situations. ([github.com](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/dev/README.md))

### Recommended code fix pattern

```java
public void updateProfile(UserProfile profile) throws SQLException {
    String sql = "UPDATE users SET name = ?, email = ? WHERE user_id = ?";

    try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
         PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

        ps.setString(1, profile.getName());
        ps.setString(2, profile.getEmail());
        ps.setString(3, profile.getUserId());

        int updated = ps.executeUpdate();
        if (updated == 0) {
            throw new SQLException("No rows updated for userId=" + profile.getUserId());
        }
    }
}
```

This pattern ensures the connection is always returned to the pool, even when an exception occurs. The `try-with-resources` change is the single most important code-level mitigation for connection pool timeout issues. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/connect/timeout-expired-error))

